<!--
  Roslyn analyzers, injected into a build WITHOUT touching the repository.

  `dotnet build -p:CustomBeforeMicrosoftCommonProps=<this file>` imports it at the top of
  Microsoft.Common.props, ahead of Directory.Build.props and of NuGet's generated props,
  which is early enough that restore collects these PackageReference items. Verified on the
  fixture: obj/project.assets.json lists Meziantou.Analyzer/3.0.224 and the build reports
  MA0074 beside the SDK's own CA1310. DirectoryBuildPropsPath was the alternative and is
  wrong here: it REPLACES a repository's own Directory.Build.props instead of adding to it.

  PrivateAssets=all: analyzers are a build-time opinion, never a dependency of the output.

  Opt out: create an empty `.qgate-no-analyzers` file next to the csproj. The build then runs
  exactly as it did before, because the properties on the command line are inert with no
  analyzer packages behind them.

  (No double hyphen anywhere in these comments: XML forbids it inside a comment, and MSBuild
  answers with MSB4024 on an import it cannot even parse.)
-->
<Project>
  <!--
    What is switched OFF, and why each one earned it. The first measurement over the three
    live repositories was 8666 diagnostics across 19 projects; nobody reads 8666. Every rule
    below was judged on SAMPLED HITS in those repositories, not on the rule's title: the
    question asked of each was "would a developer looking at this line call it a bug?".

    Roslyn resolves NoWarn per project, so this list is appended to whatever the repository
    already had ($(NoWarn) is empty in all three today) rather than replacing it. Set here
    and not on the command line so the reasoning lives beside the packages it silences.

    FALSE POSITIVES on this codebase (the rule is wrong here, not the code):
      CA1707  identifiers with underscores. These are Harmony patches: __instance, __result,
              ___privateField and __state are the injector's API, and renaming them breaks
              the patch. 134 hits, every sampled one a patch parameter.
      CA1051  visible instance fields. Unity serializes public fields; that IS the model.
      CA1401  visible P/Invoke. A mod's interop surface is deliberately public.
      MA0002  no IEqualityComparer<string> on a HashSet/Dictionary. The default already IS
              ordinal; sampled hits included a line that had just passed StringComparer.Ordinal.
      MA0046  EventHandler<T> shape. The transport's delegates are not UI events.
      CA2211/MA0069  public static mutable fields. A mod's globals are how it is reached.

    NOT DEFECTS, style or micro-performance (a hit here is never a bug report):
      MA0006  string == string. Idiomatic C#, 2391 hits over 388 files.
      MA0051  method length metric.        MA0048  file name must match type name.
      MA0047  type outside a namespace.    MA0008  StructLayoutAttribute.
      MA0016  return the abstraction.      MA0015  parameter name in ArgumentException.
      MA0023  RegexOptions.ExplicitCapture.
      CA1861  constant array argument.     CA1825  Array.Empty (MA0005 is the same rule).
      CA1859  concrete return type.        CA1805  explicit default initializer.
      CA1822  member could be static.      CA2201  throw new Exception.

    LOUD AND NOT ABOUT THIS CODE:
      CA1305/MA0011  IFormatProvider. Sampled 518 hits across all three repositories: every
              single one is a number or DateTime being concatenated into a LOG string
              (TacticalCommandSync writes the wire with BinaryWriter and only formats the
              trace line beside it). The bug this rule exists for, a float serialized with a
              comma, does not occur here. CA1310 below still catches the comparison half.
      MA0009  regex evaluation timeout. Fires on every Regex regardless of the pattern; the
              inputs are the repository's own manifests and a UPnP reply, not hostile text.

    DUPLICATES, kept once so a fix is not reported twice:
      MA0005 = CA1825, MA0011 = CA1305, MA0074 = CA1310 (identical 41 sites, CA1310 kept).

    SECOND PASS, after reading the 299 that survived: about one diagnostic in ten was
    worth reading, and these three were most of the rest.
      CA1310  culture-sensitive StartsWith / IndexOf. Kept last time on the rule's own
              merits and measured since: all 28 hits are ASCII prefix tests over the
              repositories' own manifest keys and file extensions, where the culture
              cannot change the answer. The comparison bug this rule exists for needs a
              locale-sensitive alphabet, which none of these strings has.
      CA1852  "type can be sealed". Style, and one that costs a public API change to act
              on in a mod that other mods reference.
      CA5351  MD5 is a broken hash. A JUDGEMENT CALL, and the one suppression here that
              is not simply "the rule is wrong": MD5 as a content-key digest is a correct
              use of it (ContentTool/src/Bake/CatalogText.cs:157 hashes catalog text to
              address it), MD5 for authentication is a real vulnerability, and the
              analyzer cannot tell the two apart. Silenced because every sampled hit is
              the first kind, which also means this gate will not catch the second kind.
              Said out loud here and in the README rather than left to be found.

    KEPT ON PURPOSE, including the ones still loud: MA0084 (a local hiding a field is the
    classic "assigned the wrong one" bug, 57 hits), CA1806 (an ignored return value, 18),
    MA0004 (missing ConfigureAwait), MA0134 (fire and forget), CA5350 (SHA1), CA2101
    (marshalling), and CA1001 (a type owning IDisposable fields and not disposing them),
    which is kept for ordinary C# and switched off for Unity below.

    299 to 253: Renderforge 21 to 16, ContentTool 133 to 101, Multiplayer2 145 to 136.
  -->
  <!-- No opt-out condition on this group on purpose: EnableNETAnalyzers on the command line
       still turns on the CA rules that ship inside the SDK, packages or no packages, so a
       repository holding a .qgate-no-analyzers file would otherwise get the noise back and
       none of the reasoning. -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <NoWarn>$(NoWarn);CA1707;CA1051;CA1401;CA2211;CA1305;CA1861;CA1825;CA1859;CA1805;CA1822;CA2201</NoWarn>
    <NoWarn>$(NoWarn);MA0002;MA0046;MA0069;MA0006;MA0051;MA0048;MA0047;MA0008;MA0016;MA0015</NoWarn>
    <NoWarn>$(NoWarn);MA0023;MA0005;MA0011;MA0074;MA0009</NoWarn>
    <NoWarn>$(NoWarn);CA1310;CA1852;CA5351</NoWarn>
    <!-- MA0049 type name matches its namespace: `namespace Auga { class Auga }` is the
         public API other mods reference, same class as MA0048 above. Error by default. -->
    <NoWarn>$(NoWarn);MA0049</NoWarn>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    CA1001 under Unity only. A MonoBehaviour cannot meaningfully implement IDisposable:
    Unity owns the lifecycle and calls OnDestroy, so the rule fires on every component
    that holds a disposable field and can never be satisfied. It stays ON everywhere
    else, where an undisposed IDisposable field is a real bug class.

    This retracts the earlier justification for keeping it: the "real leak" it was
    credited with, Renderforge/src/DlssDriver.cs:21 with three Unity CommandBuffers
    created at :345-348, is not a leak. DlssDriver.cs:459 calls Release() on all three
    inside TeardownResources(), reached from TryRetire at :443 on every release path.
    Read in the source, not reasoned about. Across the nine-repository sweep CA1001
    scored zero true positives: that one, and a harmless MemoryStream field in
    ContentTool/src/Bake/GlbCodec.cs:778.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup Condition="'$(QGateUnity)' == 'true'">
    <NoWarn>$(NoWarn);CA1001</NoWarn>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup Condition="!Exists('$(MSBuildProjectDirectory)\.qgate-no-analyzers')">
    <PackageReference Include="Meziantou.Analyzer" Version="3.0.224"
                      PrivateAssets="all" IncludeAssets="runtime;build;native;contentfiles;analyzers;buildtransitive" />
    <!-- Unity rules on a non-Unity project are noise, so the gate decides: it already reads
         the evaluated Reference/PackageReference list in the `refs` phase and passes
         -p:QGateUnity=true only when something there is called UnityEngine*. -->
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Unity.Analyzers" Version="1.27.0" Condition="'$(QGateUnity)' == 'true'"
                      PrivateAssets="all" IncludeAssets="runtime;build;native;contentfiles;analyzers;buildtransitive" />
    <!-- Caps the analyzer rules that default to ERROR; see the file for why and for what
         still outranks it. -->
    <GlobalAnalyzerConfigFiles Include="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)qgate.globalconfig" />
  </ItemGroup>
</Project>
